    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1351">

                <pb facs="1608" n="1410" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv02/pg1410"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">881.00/7–1845: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 1351<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Spain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d1351-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-18T13:00:00+02:00">July 18, 1945—1 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1548. British Chargé<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1351-f2"/> has been advised by
                    Castillo that Alba was last week instructed to inform British FonOff that
                    Spanish Govt considers that Russia by refusing to associate itself with
                    statutory regime in Tangier forfeited its rights under act of Algeciras and that
                    present Soviet claim to participation is consequently without juridical basis,
                    for which reason Spain cannot consent to such participation. (My 1452 July 5
                    repeated London 433 Paris 366 Tangier 79<anchor n="3" xml:id="d1351-f3"/>)
                    Castillo admitted however Spain could in fact do nothing to prevent Russian
                    participation but stated that should USSR be admitted to Tangier only course
                    open to Spain, in view of large Spanish colony and extensive Spanish interests
                    in that zone (repeated to London <pb facs="1609" n="1411" xml:id="pg1411"/> as
                    456 Paris as 398 Tangier as 82), would be to renounce statute<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d1351-f4"/> and revert to capitulatory regime.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1351-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d1351-f1-content">
                <p>The gist of this message was included in telegram No. 65 of July 19 from Grew to
                    Byrnes (file No. 800.00 Summaries/7–1945).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1351-f2" n="2" xml:id="d1351-f2-content">
                <p>Reginald James Bowker.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1351-f3" n="3" xml:id="d1351-f3-content">
                <p><ref target="frus1945Berlinv01#d688">Document No. 668, printed in vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1351-f4" n="4" xml:id="d1351-f4-content">
                <p>i. e., the regime established for Tangier by the convention signed at Paris,
                    December 18, 1923.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
